These would be target priorities, not guaranteed accuracy percentages. Actual accuracy would have to be assessed against physical measurements, historical records, or observed animal behavior.

And one more critical capability: separate the simulation from its visualization. We could run thousands of inexpensive, simplified experiments, then replay particularly informative cases as photorealistic scenes.

That is vastly more scalable than generating cinematic footage for every possible outcome.

The larger idea is that the Pantheon becomes an engine for exploring possible worlds, not just interpreting existing ones.

We could recreate the past, investigate the present, and explore counterfactual futures within one architecture—with every reconstructed world carrying its assumptions, evidence, uncertainty, and causal history.

That’s the direction I’d pursue: a Reality Forge connected to the History Stereoscope, built around the principle that we can change the camera, change the agent, or change the world itself—and observe the difference.